+# C7 Next.js frontend
+
+A **separate** Next.js 14 (App Router) frontend with Supabase SSR. It is isolated
+from the root Vite React SPA (which stays the production app) — its own
+`package.json`, `tsconfig`, and `node_modules`. Nothing here affects the root build.
+
+## Setup
+
+```bash
+cd next-frontend
+cp .env.example .env.local        # values are public/publishable keys
+npm install                       # next, react, @supabase/ssr, @supabase/supabase-js
+npm run dev                       # http://localhost:3000
+```
+
+## Structure
+
+```
+next-frontend/
+  app/
+    layout.tsx          root layout
+    page.tsx            example server component (session check)
+    globals.css
+  utils/supabase/
+    server.ts           createClient(cookieStore) for Server Components
+    client.ts           createClient() for Client Components
+    middleware.ts       updateSession() — refreshes the auth token per request
+  middleware.ts         Next middleware entry → updateSession
+  .env.example          NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL + PUBLISHABLE_KEY
+```
+
+## Notes
+
+- Env keys are **public** (publishable/anon) — safe in the client bundle. Never put
+  the service-role key here.
+- `utils/supabase/middleware.ts` calls `supabase.auth.getUser()` to actually refresh
+  the session — this was missing from the quickstart snippet and is required, or
+  sessions silently never refresh.
+- This app is **not** deployed by the root Vercel project. If you want to deploy it,
+  set its Vercel **Root Directory** to `next-frontend`.
+- Row Level Security still governs all data access; the SSR client only carries the
+  public key + the user's cookie session.
+```
