+# `mcp_query_log` RLS Fix — Design
+
+**Status:** DESIGN / REVIEW ONLY — no SQL executed, no RLS enabled, no policy
+created. Zero production change.
+**Trigger:** Supabase advisory `rls_disabled` (critical) on `public.mcp_query_log`.
+**Method:** read-only inspection (`pg_class`, grants, `pg_policy`, `pg_proc`, code grep).
+
+---
+
+## 1. Current state (confirmed read-only)
+
+| Property | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `rowsecurity` (RLS enabled) | **false** |
+| `forcerowsecurity` | false |
+| Policies | **0** |
+| Rows | ~104 (128 logged, all `success=true`; last write 2026-08-16) |
+| Columns | `id, called_at, fn_name, query, caller_role, jwt_sub, success, error_message` |
+
+The table logs MCP tool calls — including raw `query` text and `jwt_sub`
+(caller identity), so it **is** sensitive and should stay locked.
+
+## 2. Access map — the important nuance
+
+| Grantee | Table privileges | Effective SELECT |
+|---|---|---|
+| `postgres` | ALL | yes (owner) |
+| `service_role` | ALL | yes (bypasses RLS) |
+| **`anon`** | **none** | **false** |
+| **`authenticated`** | **none** | **false** |
+| `public` | none | — |
+
+**The advisory's generic wording ("fully exposed to anon and authenticated") does
+not hold here** — `anon`/`authenticated` were never granted any privilege on this
+table (`has_table_privilege('anon', …, 'SELECT') = false`). So real-world exposure
+is already nil. Enabling RLS is **defense-in-depth** and clears the advisory; it
+is not closing an active leak.
+
+## 3. Writer path (why the fix is safe)
+
+Inserts come only from:
+- **`mcp_sql_select`, `mcp_sql_execute`, `mcp_sql_delete`** — `SECURITY DEFINER`
+  functions **owned by `postgres`** → run as owner, **bypass RLS**.
+- **`service_role`** (observed `caller_role`) → **bypasses RLS**.
+
+Observed `caller_role` values: `service_role`, `none`. **No app or migration code
+references `mcp_query_log`** (code grep empty). Therefore:
+
+➡️ **Enabling RLS breaks nothing** — every writer bypasses RLS, and no reader
+depends on anon/authenticated access.
+
+## 4. Policy design
+
+Enable RLS with **no permissive policy** for anon/authenticated (deny-all to the
+public client roles). Writers (definer functions + service_role) are unaffected.
+Optionally add an **admin read** policy so a future admin UI can view the log via
+the authenticated client — included below as optional (no current consumer).
+
+## 5. Exact SQL — FOR REVIEW, NOT APPLIED
+
+```sql
+-- ⛔ REVIEW ONLY — do not execute outside an approved window.
+
+-- 5a. Enable RLS (deny-all to anon/authenticated; writers bypass RLS).
+alter table public.mcp_query_log enable row level security;
+
+-- 5b. OPTIONAL — admin read visibility (only if a future admin UI needs it).
+--     Safe to omit; service_role/postgres already read it server-side.
+-- create policy mcp_query_log_admin_read on public.mcp_query_log
+--   for select to authenticated
+--   using (public.has_role(auth.uid(), 'admin'));
+```
+
+No `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE` policy is needed — writes come from definer/service
+paths that bypass RLS. No `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` (would also constrain the
+table owner; unnecessary here).
+
+## 6. Pre-flight checks
+
+```sql
+-- No unexpected consumer relies on anon/authenticated access:
+select has_table_privilege('anon','public.mcp_query_log','SELECT')          as anon_sel,   -- expect false
+       has_table_privilege('authenticated','public.mcp_query_log','SELECT') as auth_sel;   -- expect false
+-- Writers are definer/service (bypass RLS): confirm still 3 definer fns owned by postgres.
+select proname from pg_proc p join pg_namespace n on n.oid=p.pronamespace
+ where n.nspname='public' and pg_get_functiondef(p.oid) ilike '%mcp_query_log%';
+```
+
+## 7. Post-change verification
+
+```sql
+-- (a) RLS now on, policy count as intended
+select relrowsecurity as rls_enabled,
+       (select count(*) from pg_policy where polrelid='public.mcp_query_log'::regclass) as policies
+from pg_class where oid='public.mcp_query_log'::regclass;   -- expect rls_enabled=true
+
+-- (b) Advisory cleared
+--     re-run get_advisors(type='security') → rls_disabled no longer lists mcp_query_log
+
+-- (c) Logging still works: trigger one MCP call, then confirm a new row lands
+select max(called_at) from mcp_query_log;   -- should advance after a new MCP query
+```
+
+## 8. Rollback
+
+```sql
+-- ⛔ REVIEW ONLY
+-- drop policy if the optional 5b was added:
+-- drop policy if exists mcp_query_log_admin_read on public.mcp_query_log;
+alter table public.mcp_query_log disable row level security;
+```
+
+## 9. Marker
+
+**DO NOT APPLY** without an approved window. This is design + impact analysis
+only; no RLS was enabled, no policy created, no SQL executed. Risk of the change
+is **low** (writers bypass RLS, no app reader), and it resolves the Supabase
+`rls_disabled` advisory. UI freeze baseline (`d0e80ed`) unchanged.
-- 
