+# System #7 — Security Hardening (branch-only draft)
+
+**Date:** 2026-08-22 · **Phase:** 0 → remediation draft · **Project:** `smcwrriaraptzjhqdktg`
+**Branch:** `claude/checj-s0v7tw` · **Companion to:** the consolidated readiness register (#7 AMBER).
+
+> 🔒 **Scope guard.** Branch-only draft. **No migration applied, no auth/config
+> change, no deploy, no production data touched.** Evidence is read-only (SELECT +
+> Supabase advisor + source). `real_money_enabled` ABSENT → OFF. Every remediation
+> below is **proposed**; the DB part ships as a draft migration, the Auth part as
+> change-window config actions (GoTrue settings are not SQL-migratable).
+
+Advisor findings addressed (security lints): **3× ERROR `security_definer_view`**,
+`auth_leaked_password_protection` (WARN), `auth_insufficient_mfa_options` (WARN),
+`auth_allow_anonymous_sign_ins` (WARN ×71).
+
+---
+
+## A. `security_definer_view` × 3 — DB migration draft
+
+**Views:** `public.treasury_summary`, `public.c74_reserve_status`, `public.v_igaming_ggr`.
+
+1. **Current live evidence.** All three owned by `postgres`, `reloptions = NULL`
+   (⇒ NOT `security_invoker`, run with owner privileges, **bypass RLS**), and
+   `has_table_privilege('anon', …, 'SELECT') = true` **and** for `authenticated`.
+   So anon/any signed-in user can read aggregate treasury balances
+   (`total_hot/cold/platform_funds/user_balances`), C74 reserve status, and daily
+   GGR. Regression cause: migration `20260222191842` set `security_invoker=true`
+   on `treasury_summary`; a later `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW …` reset it to definer.
+   Consumers: `admin-casino` edge fn (service_role) and `c74-status/status.js`
+   (service-role key) read them server-side; `src/hooks/useTreasury.ts` reads
+   `treasury_summary` as an **authenticated admin client**; several
+   `SECURITY DEFINER` RPCs read all three internally.
+
+2. **Security impact.** Confidentiality breach: platform financials + revenue are
+   exposed to unauthenticated/any users. High severity (ERROR).
+
+3. **Exact proposed remediation** (`supabase/migrations/20260822140000_security_definer_views_hardening.sql`):
+   ```sql
+   alter view public.treasury_summary   set (security_invoker = true);
+   alter view public.c74_reserve_status  set (security_invoker = true);
+   alter view public.v_igaming_ggr       set (security_invoker = true);
+   revoke select on public.treasury_summary  from anon;
+   revoke select on public.c74_reserve_status from anon;
+   revoke select on public.v_igaming_ggr      from anon;
+   ```
+   `ALTER VIEW … SET` changes only the option, **not** the view definition.
+   `service_role` (BYPASSRLS) keeps full visibility → admin edge/CLI reads are
+   unaffected. Anon can no longer read them at all.
+
+4. **Compatibility / breakage risk.**
+   - service_role reads (`admin-casino`, `status.js`): **no risk** (BYPASSRLS).
+   - `SECURITY DEFINER` RPCs reading the views: **no risk** (run as owner).
+   - **`useTreasury.ts` (authenticated admin client): must verify.** After
+     `security_invoker=true`, that read is RLS-scoped on the underlying tables
+     (`platform_wallets`, …). If those tables grant admins SELECT, it keeps
+     working; if not, the admin Treasury screen returns empty. **Change-window
+     step:** confirm an admin session still returns `treasury_summary`. Preferred
+     follow-up (separate change): reroute `useTreasury` to the `admin-casino`
+     service-role read, then also `REVOKE SELECT … FROM authenticated` for full
+     least-privilege. Not included here to keep the draft minimal/reviewable.
+
+5. **Rollback.** `alter view … set (security_invoker = false);` on each + re-grant
+   if ever needed (`grant select on … to anon;`). Definitions never changed, so
+   rollback is a one-line option flip. (Rolling back is **not** recommended —
+   the prior state was the vulnerability.)
+
+---
+
+## B. Leaked-password protection (`auth_leaked_password_protection`)
+
+1. **Current live evidence.** Advisor: HaveIBeenPwned check **disabled** (Auth).
+2. **Security impact.** Users can set known-compromised passwords → credential-stuffing / account takeover risk.
+3. **Exact proposed remediation (change-window config, not SQL).** Enable in
+   Supabase → **Auth → Password security → "Check against HaveIBeenPwned"** (GoTrue
+   `SECURITY_HIBP_ENABLED = true` / Management API `password_hibp_enabled: true`).
+4. **Compatibility / breakage risk.** Only blocks **new** passwords found in
+   breaches; existing sessions/passwords unaffected. Minimal; a small number of
+   sign-ups/resets may be asked to choose a different password.
+5. **Rollback.** Toggle the setting off.
+
+---
+
+## C. MFA options (`auth_insufficient_mfa_options`)
+
+1. **Current live evidence.** Advisor: too few MFA methods enabled (Auth).
+2. **Security impact.** Weak second factor for accounts — especially admin — increasing takeover risk on the money console.
+3. **Exact proposed remediation (change-window config).** Enable **TOTP**
+   (authenticator app) MFA in Supabase Auth; evaluate phone/WebAuthn per policy.
+   Application already has a separate **admin-PIN** second factor
+   (`verify-admin-pin`, `verify-two-factor-pin`) for money actions — keep it; add
+   account-level MFA on top for admin logins. Do **not** hard-enforce MFA for all
+   players in the same step (see risk).
+4. **Compatibility / breakage risk.** Enabling *availability* of TOTP is
+   non-breaking. **Enforcing** MFA for existing users without an enrollment path
+   would lock them out — stage enforcement (admins first, with enrollment)
+   separately; do not flip a global MFA-required flag in this window.
+5. **Rollback.** Disable the added MFA method / enforcement toggle.
+
+---
+
+## D. Anonymous sign-ins (`auth_allow_anonymous_sign_ins` ×71)
+
+1. **Current live evidence.** Anonymous sign-ins **enabled** at the Auth level; 71
+   table policies reference `anon`. Source check: **`signInAnonymously` has zero
+   usages** in the codebase — the app never creates anonymous auth sessions.
+   (Guest *viewing* uses the anon API **key** for public reads, which is a separate
+   mechanism and is not affected by disabling anonymous *sign-ins*.)
+2. **Security impact.** An unused-but-enabled anonymous sign-in surface lets
+   anyone mint a real (anonymous) user/JWT that satisfies `to authenticated` /
+   `anon`-scoped policies — needless attack surface.
+3. **Exact proposed remediation (change-window config).** Disable anonymous
+   sign-ins: Supabase → **Auth → Providers → Anonymous** off (GoTrue
+   `EXTERNAL_ANONYMOUS_USERS_ENABLED = false`).
+4. **Compatibility / breakage risk.** **Low** — no code path calls
+   `signInAnonymously`. Verify in the window that guest public pages (which use the
+   anon key, not anon sessions) still load. If any anonymous session is later
+   discovered in a client build, stage that first.
+5. **Rollback.** Re-enable the Anonymous provider.
+
+---
+
+## Sequencing note
+Part A ships as the draft migration `…822140000` and is applied with the other
+change-window migrations. Parts B/C/D are **Auth dashboard/Management-API config**
+changes performed in the same window (they cannot be migrations). None is applied
+here.
+
+## Confirmation
+- ✅ Branch-only draft: migration + contract test + this doc committed to the branch.
+- ✅ **No migration applied · no Auth/config change · no deploy · no production data touched.**
+- ✅ Existing RLS policies and service-role/admin functionality preserved (service_role BYPASSRLS reads intact; only anon read removed + RLS honored via security_invoker).
+- 🔒 `real_money_enabled` ABSENT → OFF.
+
+_Not legal advice._
